    isbefore: []  # fc34
    duplicate_of: []  # fc34
    not_duplicate_of: []  # fc34
    pr_ref:  # fc34
    description: "FILED 2026-09-04 after the three-step recovery was skipped in favour of hand-rolled file\nsurgery -- by an agent that had just quoted the three steps correctly. That is the signal:\nthe procedure is documented, it is short, and it still lost to improvisation, because it\ninterleaves a tracker command, a raw git command and a second tracker command.\n\nTHE STATE IT FIXES. `auto-pr` merges a PR, then its post-merge `git pull` aborts because\npending `.ops` in the working tree collide with the same paths in the incoming commit:\n\n    error: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge:\n    error: The following untracked working tree files would be overwritten by merge:\n\nThat is git correctly refusing to clobber, not a durability bug -- the ops are safe in the\nout-of-repo journal (INV-bufid). But the repo is left behind `origin/dev` with a working\ntree that no plain pull can advance, and the operator has to know a three-step dance.\n\nTHE THREE STEPS, and why the middle one is the problem: `tracker sync` (flush pending ops\nupstream) -> `git pull --ff-only` -> `tracker recover` (union-restore from the journal).\nTwo tracker commands with a raw git command between them, all three of which must be run in\norder, none of which reports whether the next one is still needed.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. An earlier sketch proposed triggering the flush with a canned harmless\nmutation repeated every time. Rejected, for three reasons found by reading the code:\n  1. UNNECESSARY. `do_sync()` takes no threshold argument -- the 80-line gate lives in\n     `_maybe_auto_sync`, its CALLER -- and `tracker sync` already exposes an unthresholded\n     flush. There is nothing to trick.\n  2. IT POLLUTES THE RECORD. A mutation writes a real op into a real item's history. The\n     tracker's value is that every line means something; synthetic ops to trip a counter\n     are permanent noise, and a sentinel item would accumulate them forever.\n  3. \"THE SAME EVERY TIME\" IS THE WORST VARIANT. Op ids are content-hashed with\n     skip-existing idempotency, so a byte-identical mutation may add no lines and fail to\n     trip the gate at all; and one mutation is ~25-35 lines against an 80-line threshold,\n     so from a low pending count it would have to loop, minting several junk ops.\n\nTHE SHAPE. A new `tracker reconcile` subcommand that runs the three steps, in order, each\nconditionally:\n  - REFUSE outright when `.git/PR_PENDING` exists. An `auto-pr` in flight is the one state\n    where this must not run (cf. WI-kifuh: auto-pr running a tracker mutation inside its own\n    gate, and auto-sync then waiting 15 minutes).\n  - Flush only if ops are actually pending; fast-forward only if actually behind; recover\n    only if the journal holds something the tree lacks. A clean no-op when all three are\n    already true, so it is safe to run reflexively after every `auto-pr`.\n  - Do NOT set the `tracker-recover-disabled` marker around the fast-forward. `do_sync`\n    sets it around its own fetch/ff deliberately; here the `post-checkout` and\n    `reference-transaction` hooks should stay live so they can self-heal (cf. WI-pohir: a\n    leaked marker silently disables self-healing and nothing detects it).\n  - REPORT pending counts before and after. \"Did it actually flush\" was the question that\n    could not be answered without counting files by hand -- and counting them with `ls`\n    returns a FALSE ZERO, because `.ops` are dotfiles.\n\nTHEN DEPRECATE `tracker recover`. It stays as the primitive `reconcile` calls, but the\ndocumented entry point becomes `reconcile`, and AGENTS.md / the playbooks that name\n`recover` are updated to match. `recover` alone is a partial answer: it restores the journal\nbut leaves the repo behind, which is how the operator ends up improvising the other two\nsteps.\n\nNOT DONE HERE: no sweep for other places the three-step dance is documented; the doc update\nis part of the work, not a precondition."  # fc34
    fields: {}  # fc34
