- op: discuss  # f917
  at: '2026-09-04T22:31:21Z'  # f917
  by: agent  # f917
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f917
  clock: 4  # f917
  nonce: f917  # f917
  message: "DONE. `tracker reconcile` ships; `tracker recover` is deprecated to the primitive it always\nwas. Closing with behavioural evidence rather than a proxy, per the closure-evidence\ndiscipline: this item's statement is about what an operator can RUN, so it is closed on runs.\n\nSHIPPED IN PR #780 (dev 6a9a0a317f). Re-run on the MERGED tree, against the real\nrepository, not on a fixture:\n\n    $ ./scripts/tracker reconcile --dry-run\n    reconcile: would flush 2 file(s)\n    reconcile: would fast-forward to the upstream\n    reconcile: nothing to restore (journal: .../tracker-ops-journal/8b85be80f37d)\n    rc=0\n\n    $ ./scripts/tracker recover\n    recover: DEPRECATED - use `tracker reconcile`, which also flushes pending ops and\n    fast-forwards. ...\n\nIt found the 2 genuinely-pending ops rather than reporting a comfortable zero, and it\nresolved the main checkout's journal.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE FROM DEVELOPMENT, both paths:\n\n    $ hypergumbo-tracker reconcile --dry-run\n    reconcile: nothing to flush\n    reconcile: would fast-forward to the upstream\n    reconcile: nothing to restore (journal: .../tracker-ops-journal/54d25ad27513)\n    rc=0\n\n    $ hypergumbo-tracker reconcile          # real, on a branch with no upstream\n    reconcile: nothing to flush\n    reconcile: fast-forward did not run:\n    There is no tracking information for the current branch.\n    ...\n\nThe second is the one worth reading. The command declined to claim success and printed the\nVCS's own words, which is the property the design turns on: a silent step is\nindistinguishable from a step that was not needed, and that ambiguity is exactly what sends\nthe next reader back to doing it by hand.\n\n    $ hypergumbo-tracker recover\n    recover: DEPRECATED — use `tracker reconcile`, which also flushes pending ops and\n    fast-forwards. `recover` restores the journal and leaves the repo behind, which is how\n    the other two steps get skipped.\n    recover: nothing to restore (...)\n\nTHE CANNED-MUTATION DESIGN IS REFUTED, not merely declined — and refuting it is the part of\nthis item that will matter to whoever reads it next. `do_sync()` takes NO threshold\nargument; the line-count gate lives in `_maybe_auto_sync`, its CALLER. So a synthetic\nmutation would have been minting real ops into a real item's history to trip a counter that\ndoes not gate the function being called. \"The same every time\" was the worst variant:\nop ids are content-hashed with skip-existing idempotency, so a byte-identical mutation may\nadd no lines and fail to trip the gate at all.\n\nWHAT THE TESTS PIN (13 new, in test_reconcile.py; the package's full 2856 pass):\n  - refuses under .git/PR_PENDING (an in-flight auto-pr moves the ops dirs around its own\n    rebase; the two would fight over the same files)\n  - a FAILED FLUSH DOES NOT FAST-FORWARD, propagating do_sync's exit code — advancing on\n    top of ops that never reached the remote is the collision this exists to prevent\n  - leaves no `tracker-recover-disabled` marker: do_sync sets it around its own fetch on\n    purpose, but here the self-healing hooks must stay LIVE, since restoring ops the pull\n    disturbs is their job\n  - a clean no-op on a healthy repo, which is what makes it safe to run reflexively after\n    every auto-pr — the only way it gets run at all\n  - a refusing preflight does not strand the later steps; a repo whose only real need was\n    its journal back still gets it\n\nVERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, and it changed the outcome: `.githooks/reference-transaction`\nand `.githooks/post-checkout` both invoke `./scripts/tracker recover >/dev/null 2>&1 ||\ntrue`. Stderr is discarded, so the deprecation notice is silent on every automatic\ninvocation and surfaces only for manual use. Had they not redirected, this would have\nshipped a deprecation warning printing on every ref transaction and every checkout in the\nrepo. No `.githooks/**` change was needed.\n\nDOCS: AGENTS.md's Op Durability bullet now names `reconcile` in all four places it named\n`recover` as the thing you run by hand, plus a new sentence stating that `reconcile` is the\nanswer to the aborted-pull state and that the three-step recipe is superseded. Three\n`recover` mentions deliberately remain (what the hooks call, the deprecation note, the\ndescription of the old recipe). Governance edit made under explicit owner approval.\n`journal.py`'s docstring and the tracker CHANGELOG updated; the root CHANGELOG's two\nmentions are historical released entries and were left alone.\n\nNOT DONE, stated rather than papered over:\n  - No sweep for other places the three-step dance may be written down; only AGENTS.md and\n    journal.py were found by grepping for `tracker recover`.\n  - `journal.recover()` keys the journal on the repo TOPLEVEL, so running `reconcile` from\n    a git WORKTREE resolves a different journal directory than the main checkout. That is\n    pre-existing `recover` behaviour, not introduced here, and it is not addressed. It was\n    observed directly during this work (the worktree reported journal 54d25ad27513 where\n    the main checkout uses 8b85be80f37d)."  # f917
